Checkers registered for ``For`` and ``With`` now also run on ``async for`` and
``async with``. The AST walker dispatches on the node's exact class name, so
``visit_for`` never received an ``AsyncFor`` even though ``AsyncFor`` is a
subclass of ``For``. This makes ``useless-else-on-loop``,
``confusing-with-statement``, ``redeclared-assigned-name``,
``redefined-argument-from-local`` and ``too-many-nested-blocks`` fire on the
async forms.

``unnecessary-dict-index-lookup``, ``unnecessary-list-index-lookup`` and
``unbalanced-dict-unpacking`` reach their ``async for`` handler now as well, but
all three first have to infer a dict, a list or ``enumerate()`` as the iterated
object, and none of those is an async iterator, so in practice they only apply
to code that already raises ``TypeError`` at runtime.

Closes #11417
